Platform Goals:

  =>Support 250+ engineers across 20–50+ teams.
  =>Enable true multi-tenancy (team isolation + shared efficiency).
  =>Provide self-service CI/CD.
  =>Enforce security, compliance, and standards by default.
  =>Scale horizontally with minimal platform team effort

#Multi-tenancy isolation strategy and security :

Step 1: Define the Tenant Model

 Decision: Engineering Team = 1 Tenant
    Tools:
       *GitHub / GitLab groups
       * Okta / Azure AD / Google Workspace
 Result:
  * Each team has its own identity group
  *Platform automation uses this group to create access, namespaces, and pipelines

Step 2: Central Identity & Access:

Tools
  * Okta / Azure AD (SSO)
  * GitHub / GitLab (OIDC)
  * AWS IAM (or GCP IAM / Azure RBAC)

Purpose:
  * Engineers log in using SSO
  * No shared accounts
  * CI/CD uses short-lived credentials via OIDC


Step 3: Source Code Isolation:

Tools
  * GitHub / GitLab / Bitbucket
Purpose:
  * Each team owns:
  * Their repos 
  * Folders with ownership rules.
  * Branch protection enforced
What we acheived using this:
  * Teams don’t accidentally change each other’s code
  * Ownership is clear

Step 4: CI Pipeline Isolation

Tools
  * GitHub Actions / GitLab CI
  * Self-hosted runners (Kubernetes-based)
Purpose:
    * Each team pipeline:
    * Runs with its own identity
    * Uses scoped permissions
    * Sensitive workloads use dedicated runners
    * One team’s pipeline cannot deploy or access another team’s resources

Step 5: Secure Build & Scan:
Tools
    * Trivy / Snyk (container & dependency scanning)
    * SonarQube (static code quality)
Purpose:
    * Every build is scanned automatically
    * Policies enforced before merge or deploy
Implementation results:
    * Security is automatic, not manual
    * Unsafe code never reaches production

Step 6: Artifact Storage & Trust
Tools
    * Amazon ECR / GCR / ACR
    * Helm Registry (OCI)
Purpose:
   * Only approved registries are allowed
   * Images are immutable and signed
Implementation:
   *Prevents tampered or unknown images from running

Step 7: Deployment via GitOps (Controlled CD)
Tools
    * Argo CD / Flux
    * Git repositories for environments
Purpose:
    * Teams commit desired state to Git
    * Argo CD deploys only what’s approved
    * Drift is automatically detected
Results:
    * No manual kubectl in production
    * Full audit trail of changes

Step 8: Runtime Isolation 
Tools
   * Kubernetes
   * Namespaces
   * ResourceQuotas & LimitRanges

Purpose:
   * Each team gets:
     * team-a-dev
     * team-a-staging
     * team-a-prod
* Resource usage is assgined per team
Why:
     * One team can’t exhaust cluster resources
     * Failures stay contained

Step 9: Network Isolation
Tools
    * Kubernetes NetworkPolicies
    * Calico / Cilium
    * Istio 
What happens
    * Default deny all traffic
    * Explicit allow:
    * Ingress → App
    * App → DB
    * App → shared services
Purpose:
* Zero-trust networking
* Stops lateral movement across teams

Step 10: Secrets & Sensitive Data

Tools
  * HashiCorp Vault
  * AWS Secrets Manager / GCP Secret Manager
  * Kubernetes External Secrets
What we can achieved by these:
* Secrets are:
    * Never stored in Git
    * Scoped to a team’s namespace
    * Automatically rotated keys
Reason for implemention
    * Strong data isolation
    * No credential leaks

Step 11: Observability 
Tools
   * Prometheus + Grafana (metrics)
   * Loki / ELK (logs)/splunk
   * OpenTelemetry (tracing)
   * Kubecost (cost visibility)
Purpose:
* Teams can see their
    * logs
    * metrics
    * costs
    * traces
* Platform team sees everything


Step 12: Audit & Incident Control
Tools
   * Kubernetes Audit Logs
   * CloudTrail / Cloud Logging
   * SIEM (Splunk / Datadog)
Purpose:
   * Every action is logged
   * Incidents are traceable and contained
   * Fast root cause analysis
   * Strong compliance posture


#Scalability approach (20 → 50+ teams:

Scaling from 20 → 50+ teams is achieved by:
  *Adding clusters
  *Increasing automation
  *Strengthening isolation

Tool:CI/CD

Things to be implemented:

  *GitHub Actions / GitLab CI
  *Mostly shared runners
  *Namespace-scoped deploys

Tool: Kubernetes Clusters
Thngs we achieved using these:
  *1 Non-Prod Cluster
  *1–2 Prod Clusters
  *Namespace per team
  *NetworkPolicies for isolation
  *Shared ingress & services

| Dimension    | ~20 Teams             | 50+ Teams                         |
| ------------ | --------------------- | --------------------------------- |
| Clusters     | 1–2 shared clusters   | Multiple clusters per environment |
| Isolation    | Namespace-level       | Cluster + namespace               |
| CI/CD        | Mostly shared runners | Tiered + dedicated runners        |
| Blast Radius | Medium                | Small                             |
| Scaling Unit | Nodes                 | Clusters                          |
| Ops Load     | Manual + automated    | Fully automated                   |


